artifact.self_contained_problem:
For a scalar-valued function $f:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{C}$ that is Stepanov bounded of exponent $r\in[1,\infty)$, recall that $f$ belongs to the Stepanov-type almost periodic space $AP^r(\mathbb{R},\mathbb{C})$ if it is the limit, in the Stepanov norm
$$\|g\|_{S^r} \;=\; \sup_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \Bigl(\int_t^{t+1} |g(s)|^r\,ds\Bigr)^{1/r},$$
of a sequence of trigonometric polynomials $\sum_{k=1}^{N} a_k e^{i\lambda_k t}$ with $a_k\in\mathbb{C}$ and $\lambda_k\in\mathbb{R}$. Equivalently, $f\in AP^r(\mathbb{R},\mathbb{C})$ iff the set of its Stepanov $\varepsilon$-almost periods
$$\{\tau\in\mathbb{R} : \|f(\cdot+\tau)-f(\cdot)\|_{S^r}<\varepsilon\}$$
is relatively dense in $\mathbb{R}$ for every $\varepsilon>0$.

Let $X$ be an arbitrary (complex) Banach space. Define the corresponding space $AP^r(\mathbb{R},X)$ of $X$-valued Stepanov-type almost periodic functions by replacing $|\cdot|$ with $\|\cdot\|_X$ in the definitions above, and replacing scalar coefficients $a_k$ by elements of $X$ in the approximating trigonometric polynomials.

Problem: Develop a complete theory of the spaces $AP^r(\mathbb{R},X)$ extending the scalar-valued case. In particular, establish:
(i) the equivalence (or precise relationship) between the approximation definition and the relative-density-of-almost-periods definition in the $X$-valued setting;
(ii) completeness, structural and Banach-module properties of $AP^r(\mathbb{R},X)$;
(iii) a Bohr–Fourier series representation with $X$-valued Fourier coefficients, together with uniqueness, Parseval-type identities (where applicable), and a characterization of the set of Bohr–Fourier exponents;
(iv) the role of the geometry of $X$ (reflexivity, Radon–Nikodým property, type/cotype, UMD) in obtaining the analogues of classical theorems (Bochner's criterion, mean-value theorem, approximation theorems, behaviour of indefinite integrals);
(v) the relationship of $AP^r(\mathbb{R},X)$ to the Bohr space $AP(\mathbb{R},X)$ of uniformly almost periodic $X$-valued functions and to the Weyl and Besicovitch $X$-valued analogues.
